{
  "platform_id": "U5vJ3hlopUk",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:U5vJ3hlopUk",
    "purpose": "Shows how to make Kenji's extra-crusty burger: hand-chopping a blend of beef cuts, handling and salting the meat to keep it loose, then cooking single patties in a small hot pan. It includes a side-by-side test of salting before versus after forming patties.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Grind whole cuts of beef yourself just before cooking, handle the meat as little as possible, and salt only the outside right before it hits the pan. This gives a loose, craggly patty with maximum crust and juiciness.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Different beef cuts have different flavors; chuck alone is balanced, and this burger uses about 75% chuck and 25% short rib.",
      "Meat can be ground in a grinder, pulsed in a food processor after partially freezing it, or chopped by hand with a heavy cleaver.",
      "Keep meat cold while grinding or chopping. If the fat starts to smear, chill the meat on a sheet tray in the freezer for a few minutes.",
      "Salting and kneading meat before forming the patty cross-links the proteins and makes a tight, rubbery, sausage-like burger. Salt only the exterior just before cooking.",
      "Form patties loosely with ragged edges to maximise surface area and crust.",
      "Cook in a very hot carbon steel, cast iron or stainless pan, not nonstick. Use a pan just big enough for the patties so the burger fries in its own rendered fat.",
      "Top with thinly sliced onions and American cheese. Serve on a soft, cheap white bun with a sauce of mayo, ketchup (about 3:1), relish and a pinch of sugar."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The science of burger meat",
        "summary": "Introduces the old-fashioned burger recipe and explains how different beef cuts contribute different flavors, like notes in a chord. Chuck is the best single cut.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Hand-grinding the beef",
        "summary": "Trims chuck and short rib and covers grinder and food processor options. Then hand-chops with a cleaver, chilling the meat whenever the fat starts to smear.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Forming the burger patties",
        "summary": "Forms a patty by barely touching the meat with a bench scraper. Compares meat to bread dough, which tightens the more it is worked.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "The impact of salting timing",
        "summary": "Makes a second patty salted and kneaded before forming. Explains how salt dissolves proteins and creates a bouncy, sausage-like texture.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Cooking and testing patties",
        "summary": "Sears the pre-salted and loosely formed patties side by side in a carbon steel pan and warns against nonstick at high heat. Compares the crust and then throws both patties to show their texture.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 22
      },
      {
        "title": "Cooking the perfect burger",
        "summary": "Salts and peppers only the exterior of a loose patty and cooks it alone in a small, lightly oiled pan so it fries in its own fat.",
        "first_segment": 23,
        "last_segment": 25
      },
      {
        "title": "Sauce and assembly",
        "summary": "Mixes a special sauce and gives tips on managing smoke. Flips the burger, tops it with onions and American cheese, and assembles it on a soft white bun.",
        "first_segment": 25,
        "last_segment": 29
      },
      {
        "title": "Tasting and conclusion",
        "summary": "Tastes the burger, points out the loose texture with pockets of fat and crisp bits, and recaps the key rules.",
        "first_segment": 29,
        "last_segment": 32
      }
    ],
